2.	AI PROBLEM FRAMING
2.1	Real world Problem

The real world problem that is occurring is the difficulties on identifying the students that may be struggling on their study early so that an appropriate academic guidance can be given.

As an example, a student may show a gradual decline in their study that may have not be shown in early and middle of the semester but it will be detected after the final examination of the semester is complete. This show that monitoring the academic grade on the test and assessment during early and middle semester alone cannot guarantee the students can be identified early.

The easy way to express this is the academic staff need a practical support to identify the struggling students so that they can be given appropriate guidance and the support they need early on before the decline of their study.

2.2	Current Process

The current process of identifying student who may have academic difficulties often relies on lecturer manually reviewing students academic performance such as examination marks, assessment results and attendance record. Lecturers then use this information and their professional judgement to identify students who may need support.

However, manually reviewing student records can be time consuming and put a heavy strain on lecturer or academic staff body particularly when they need to handle a large number of students. Different lecturer may have different method of identifying at risk student, this may lead to inconsistent identification. In addition, student who show early signs of academic decline may not always be identified promptly. These challenges and problem indicate a need for a more systematic approach to help the lecturer identify the student who may need help.

2.3	Value of Solving the Problem

2.3.1	Early Identification

Protentional struggling students could be identified early before their academic performance decline even further.

2.3.2	Reduced Manual Screening

The Lecturers could be help by reducing the burden of manual labor on identifying and reviewing the students personally one by one.

2.3.3	Consistent Screening

Help the lecturer to quickly give their focus directly on the struggling students.

2.3.4	Evidence-informed decisions

The lecturer can use the recommendation and the academic information provided by the AI-assisted system’s analysis to decide whether the students need further review.

2.3.5	Better resource Allocation

The spare time that is provided with the help of the AI-assisted system can be used by the academic staff to help more students who also need support.


2.4	Potential Harm

2.4.1	False Negative
The student who genuinely need the support they need can be classified as the lowest priority.

•	Potential Harm
The Students may decline more on their study without proper guidance.


2.4.2	False Positive
The students who performed positively may be classified as highest priority.

•	Potential Harm
The students may lost their confident because of being incorrectly classified as academically weak and need support. Their study may decline.


2.4.3	Privacy Risk
The academic information of the students may leak and resulting in unauthorised and inappropriate use of information.

2.4.4	Overreliance Risk
The academic staff may overuse and incorrectly treat the AI recommendation as the final decision and just follow the AI decision.


2.5	Non-Ai Alternative

Non-AI uses simple rule to identify student who need guidance and support from the lecturer and academic advisors. If a student attendance falls below a certain level, the system automatically flagged the student for academically at risk. However, low attendance may not necessary be stdeunt are struggling in their study.

Similarly, if a student’s assessment fall below a certain score, the system shall flags for review. However, a single low score in an assessment may not accurately reflect student academic performance.

The system also flags student who did not submit their assignment but student may have legitimate reason as to not submitting their work. Finally, the system identifies student whose academic performance have decline and flags them for review. This simple identification may not have capture a more complex patterns in student academic performance  

These rules may be easy to follow and understand. But fixed rules may have not capture a more complex combination of academic indicators. So, AI approach should be compared to this non-AI baseline rather than assuming AI recommendation is just.
